WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.920
Mix up centuries, politics, and maps.&nbsp;
Throw in a little narrative confusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.500
and finish it off with some over-the-top CGI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.700
madness. King Arthur just can’t catch a&nbsp;
break onscreen, but could there be hope?

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.380
Many adaptations place King Arthur in&nbsp;
a vague, medieval-fantasy-ish England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.260
perhaps because it's easier&nbsp;
than explaining the historical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.680
political, and cultural realities&nbsp;
of their version of the character.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:24.840
"Welcome to Camelot."

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.140
But this reliance on obscurity&nbsp;
creates its own mess of problems.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.720
Arthur's origins are British — not English. By&nbsp;
British, we mean the various Brittonic-speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.340
Celts of Sub-Roman Britain that would come to&nbsp;
be known as "Cymry." These are primarily the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.920
modern-day Welsh, Cornish, and Breton. And by&nbsp;
English, we mean the various Germanic tribes:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.300
primarily the Saxons, Jutes, and Angles&nbsp;
that would come to be known as "English."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.980
Many scholars believe that if Arthur was&nbsp;
a single individual who actually existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.960
he would have been part of Sub-Roman Britain's&nbsp;
5th and 6th century resistance to Saxon invasion.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.940
Here's the thing: the Irish, or "Scots" at&nbsp;
the time, and the Picts of modern-day Scotland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.840
were also taking advantage of the fractured,&nbsp;
weakened state in which Rome's withdrawal had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.540
left Arthur's Britain. Surrounded by threats on&nbsp;
all sides, the odds were insurmountable. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.260
historical evidence suggests that for&nbsp;
at least a couple generations, someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.600
possibly even a guy named Arthur, was able to&nbsp;
unite his "countrymen," fend off these enemies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.700
and bring peace and stability to the region.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.260
None of this means that Arthur hasn't been&nbsp;
extremely important to English identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:33.720
or that he can't be depicted as English. It simply&nbsp;
means that screenwriters have to decide who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.580
or what, their Arthur will be fighting. The&nbsp;
issue isn't that so many adaptations get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.500
tradition or history "wrong," but that so few&nbsp;
of them see this decision as integral to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.540
story. Without "insurmountable odds" and a&nbsp;
lost cause, Arthur's story has no meaning.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.180
John Boorman's "Excalibur" doesn't trouble itself&nbsp;
with such decisions. Instead, Boorman stuffs his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.740
movie with as many King Arthur allegories as he&nbsp;
can, but no tangible enemy for most of the film.

00:01:59.142 --> 00:02:01.362
"Where hides evil then, in my kingdom?"

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:05.141
That leaves us with one of the least&nbsp;
compelling Arthurs in recent history.

00:02:05.949 --> 00:02:10.380
It's understandably tricky to make Arthur's "big&nbsp;
bad" the Saxons in a narrative that positions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:15.000
him as an English hero, but the protagonists&nbsp;
and villains of Guy Ritchie's "King Arthur:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.680
Legend of the Sword" are so vague that it's&nbsp;
difficult to care about either faction. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.340
the linguistic mishmash and laddish&nbsp;
flash-forwards feel arbitrary and out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.360
of place in this CGI-reliant tornado of&nbsp;
fantasy vomit. But they're not even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.960
worst aspect of "Legend of the Sword."&nbsp;
Sadly, they might be the best part.

00:02:31.101 --> 00:02:33.621
"Now tell me a story about a girl called Lucy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.621 --> 00:02:37.620
a Viking called Grey Beard, and some&nbsp;
rebel graffiti dirtying these walls."

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:38.920
"Are you writing a book?"

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.560
The film isn't set in any particular,&nbsp;
real-life era or place. That could work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.100
except that the world we are given&nbsp;
is so scattered and generic in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.360
"otherworldly-ness" that we can't place,&nbsp;
relate to, or derive any meaning from it.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.820
Legends like Arthur endure not because of their&nbsp;
fantasy, but because of our collective belief in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.380
and need for, their reality. We want to&nbsp;
believe in Arthur as if he's a real person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:06.060
and while that doesn't mean he needs to be&nbsp;
"realistic," it does mean we need to see our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.380
real-world hopes and struggles reflected&nbsp;
in his story. Ritchie's world provides no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.700
such reflection. There's a universe in which&nbsp;
Ritchie's film comments on the abusive power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.876
that the wealthy wield over the working&nbsp;
class, but that universe is not this one.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:24.300
Which brings us to writer David Franzoni and&nbsp;
director Antoine Fuqua's pile of anachronistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:30.120
chaos, 2004's "King Arthur." Fuqua's mistake isn't&nbsp;
that he keeps things too vague. It's that he tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.280
to be specific and "accurate," but in the most&nbsp;
confusing manner possible. We can forgive the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.660
film for calling the Picts "woads," and for&nbsp;
dressing said woads in leather nipple belts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.460
We can even forgive it for weaving 12th century&nbsp;
story additions into its 5th century narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.960
since frankly, no one wants to see an&nbsp;
Arthur without a Merlin and a Lancelot.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.200
The rest of this, however,&nbsp;
is historically unforgivable.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.280
The film opens with the words,&nbsp;
"Historians agree that..." — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.420
there's our first issue. Historians don't&nbsp;
agree on anything, least of all Arthur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.680
and this inaccurate and inadequate appeal&nbsp;
to authority is a sign of things to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.300
Not only does Franzoni have a Roman elite&nbsp;
living several days north of Hadrian's Wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.700
he also has Saxon tribes invading Britain from&nbsp;
the northwest corner of modern-day Scotland. Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.140
these 5th century Saxons come from Iceland?&nbsp;
Was their map upside-down? We can't know.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.260
There are other historical&nbsp;
and geographical issues here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.265
but they pale in comparison to the film's&nbsp;
confused understanding of Sub-Roman Britain.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.500
In the 2004 film, Clive Owen's Arthur gallops&nbsp;
around shouting about personal freedom while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.580
attempting to free his warriors of their service,&nbsp;
and poor farmers from the lands they serve.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.940
"These are our orders. We leave&nbsp;
at first light and when we return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.800
your freedom will be waiting for you."

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.640
What this Arthur is effectively doing&nbsp;
is ensuring these people have no food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.660
no community, no protection in&nbsp;
times of war, and nowhere to live.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.100
There are so many issues with this film,&nbsp;
including its half-baked use of C. Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:58.320
Littleton and Ann C. Thomas' Sarmatian Connection,&nbsp;
its complete misrepresentation of Pelagianism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.640
and its wacky portrayal of both the&nbsp;
Romano-British and the Picts. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.900
there's a universe in which Arthur as a champion&nbsp;
of inherent, individual freedom makes sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.940
but that universe is 18th century France&nbsp;
— not 5th century Rome or Britain.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.720
That said, the fact that "King Arthur"&nbsp;
at least attempts to give its leader a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.860
cause places it firmly above STARZ's&nbsp;
failed medieval series, "Camelot."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.380
In this accidental parody of itself, Eva&nbsp;
Green's Morgan exists solely to glare, seduce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.720
and serve as a warning about the dangers of female&nbsp;
ambition. We're given no reason to care about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.120
series' tween-ish Arthur, save the fact that he&nbsp;
is Arthur, or for the one-dimensional Guinevere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.860
It's possible "Camelot" had redemption arcs in&nbsp;
mind, but you can't build on a busted foundation.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.180
"Camelot," like the vast majority of&nbsp;
all on-screen Arthurian adaptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.020
is a bit like watching a first grader&nbsp;
recite Shakespeare. The words are there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:54.120
but the comprehension and delivery are not.&nbsp;
Ironically, they usually are there in productions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.860
aimed at younger audiences. Adaptations such as&nbsp;
Disney's "The Sword in the Stone," for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:03.180
benefit from being forced to think about what&nbsp;
the message is in the process of relaying it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:07.800
more simply. Arthurian parodies benefit&nbsp;
from this deconstruction process as well.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.000
"Who are the Britons?"

00:06:09.382 --> 00:06:12.622
"Well, we all are. We are all&nbsp;
Britons. And I am your king."

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.260
"Didn't know we had a king. I thought&nbsp;
we were an autonomous collective."

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.040
When more serious adaptations have managed&nbsp;
to strike the right balance of subtext,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.580
context, history, and plot, it's&nbsp;
almost always because they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.480
chosen to focus on a single character&nbsp;
or storyline from the wider tradition.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:33.180
This is why Jerry Zucker's "First Knight" isn't&nbsp;
one of the worst Arthurian movies ever made. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.500
the very least, "First Knight" focuses on a&nbsp;
single element of the legend, and is fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.940
clear about what its Arthur is attempting&nbsp;
to create. It also provides a nuanced and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:46.980
relatively historically informed understanding of&nbsp;
Chrétien de Troyes' famous courtly love triangle.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:52.320
Does this mean adaptations can't succeed if they&nbsp;
attempt to tell the whole story? No. At least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.218
not if they use their source material wisely.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.200
You'd be hard-pressed to find a version&nbsp;
of the Arthurian legend that's truer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.160
its historical and cultural origins than&nbsp;
Bernard Cornwell's Warlord Chronicles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.660
The trilogy is so fully embedded in what&nbsp;
little history we know of the time period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.860
and so thoroughly informed by Brythonic&nbsp;
mythology, that it's easy to forget that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.060
these are works of fiction written in the&nbsp;
'90s. The first-hand account we're given is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:19.200
through the eyes of an aging pseudo-monk named&nbsp;
Derfel, who was once Arthur's loyal friend.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.060
Every time Derfel reveals a gap between the&nbsp;
legend and his lived experiences, we get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.100
impression that for the first time, we actually&nbsp;
are hearing "the real story." Cornwell's trilogy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:33.960
finds a way to historically re-position and ground&nbsp;
the well-known elements of Arthurian legend. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.940
an effective, and easily adaptable, structure.&nbsp;
And since Cornwall has already done the research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:43.500
required to resolve the historical Arthur with&nbsp;
the literary one, Bad Wolf's upcoming television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.880
adaptation won't have to wrestle with that aspect&nbsp;
unless it decides not to trust its audience.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.860
It also won't have to decide what its Arthur&nbsp;
represents. Cornwell's Arthur stories are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.120
political in nature, meaning that they speak&nbsp;
to the political mechanics of a "just society."

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:03.120
"What I do now, I do for my&nbsp;
people, and for Camelot."

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.700
In the trilogy's first novel, "The Winter King,"&nbsp;
Cornwell tells us exactly how his Arthur thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:13.920
society should operate. Arthur believes "a king is&nbsp;
only as good as the poorest man under his rule,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.200
and that the strong are duty-bound to protect&nbsp;
the weak. He believes justice can't exist unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.720
oaths are kept, and the laws of a land apply to&nbsp;
every citizen. Battles should be fought only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:29.760
secure peace, and he never creates a round table&nbsp;
of perfect knights — only a Brotherhood of Britain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.620
that relies on various leaders' putting old&nbsp;
grudges to rest in the name of keeping that peace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.460
He's not a king, but a warrior sworn&nbsp;
to secure the kingdom of Dumnonia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:43.800
or modern-day Devon and Cornwall, until the&nbsp;
infant King Mordred comes of age. Arthur's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.740
goal is to unite the warring tribes&nbsp;
and kingdoms of Britain so they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:50.700
defend themselves against the Saxons, the&nbsp;
Irish, and the raiding northern tribes.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.380
It's not particularly romantic to think&nbsp;
of Arthur as a "politician," but in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.640
broadest sense of the term, that's precisely&nbsp;
what he has to become in order to secure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:04.800
maintain the peace. Like most politicians,&nbsp;
Arthur's personal ambitions are in constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.480
conflict with his political ideals, which are&nbsp;
themselves in constant conflict with reality.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:15.960
Cornwell's entire narrative is a story of lenses&nbsp;
and of tensions, both within the individual and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:21.180
society as a whole. Nowhere are these tensions&nbsp;
more apparent than in Arthur's embodiment of them.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.060
For all his lofty ideology, Cornwell's Arthur&nbsp;
himself breaks oaths, fights battles in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.440
name of things other than peace, and can't&nbsp;
help but enjoy the thrill of battle. He may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.620
be an idealist, but he lives, as we do, in an&nbsp;
un-ideal world. That world is ugly, brutal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.620
and continually tests his strength — not with CGI&nbsp;
monsters or a crisis of Christian faith — but with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:49.680
a crisis of faith in himself, and by extension,&nbsp;
humanity. Like every society that's ever lived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:54.420
Arthur's triumphs and defeats are cyclical. But&nbsp;
to believe in Arthur is to believe that a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:59.220
version of humanity is possible despite this&nbsp;
cycle, even when the odds are insurmountable.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.600
We don't keep resurrecting "the once&nbsp;
and future king" because he represents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.200
a pretty version of the "once," but&nbsp;
because he inspires us to fight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:12.660
the "future." Fantasy Arthur can't do&nbsp;
that in 2023, but Cornwell's Arthur can.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.940
In the second novel, Derfel argues how&nbsp;
powerful ideals in an un-ideal world can be:

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.620
"To hear the tales told at night-time hearths&nbsp;
you would think we had made a whole new country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:27.600
in Britain, named it Camelot and peopled it&nbsp;
with shining heroes, but the truth is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.820
we simply ruled Dumnonia as best we could,&nbsp;
we ruled it justly and we never called it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:38.640
Camelot. [...] Camelot exists only in the poets'&nbsp;
dreams, while in our Dumnonia, even in those good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:43.860
years, the harvests still failed, the plagues&nbsp;
still ravaged us and wars were still fought."

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:49.140
It's easy to believe in Camelot. It's&nbsp;
infinitely harder, and infinitely more heroic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:52.380
to believe in and fight for the&nbsp;
world in which we actually live.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.060
Now we just have to hope this adaptation&nbsp;
sees the romance in an Arthur who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:01.020
through his wholly unromantic humanity,&nbsp;
can actually speak to that world.

